## Matcha-Flavored

Complaining about Minecraft and Mojang is one of the most widespread and
time-honored traditions of Minecraft players, and I'm no exception. Neither is
Klei_wright, but he actually went and did something about it, and produced an
amazing minecraft rework, the
[Matcha-Flavored](https://modrinth.com/project/QI0EmgZ1) datapack.

It really does feel like he rewound the clock back to the earliest versions of
minecraft and then played it forward through a very different timeline, ending
up with something that simultaniously feels more Minecraft than most 'vanilla
plus' packs and entirely foreign; with changes ranging from removing the whole
hunger and xp systems, to making small flowers duplicatable through crafting.

I do have my issues with it, make no mistake, but if I ever get around to making
my own minecraft rework it'll likely be built on Matcha, not vanilla Minecraft.

Replacing hunger directly with eating to heal and merging potion effects into
the regular food system was a great move, and I actually enjoyed going through
and making as wide a selection of foods as I could. Which is not something I've
ever done in mods like farmer's delight. Normally I find what I consider to be
the best saturation per effort food in jei and only ever make and eat that, but
in Matcha I found myself maintaining a selection of foods for their potion
effects or to use up farm produce even after unlocking all the recipes and
deciding on a bulk food source.

I similarly approve of the progression and material changes, like dropping stone
tools altogether in favor of integrating copper properly and doubling iron
crafting products if you use steel. But I hear there's been some more
significant changes since I last played, so I'll hold off on farther comments
there.

Replacing redstone with copper was a similarly inspired move, freeing up an ore
for other purposes, making it easier to get redstone components, and giving
copper more uses in one fell swoop.

I didn't reach the endgame gear, but in principle I agree that having craftable
enchants is greatly preferable to villager halls or random rolls. I'm not a fan
of bringing in real world religious pluralism as flavortext; it throws me out of
the game. I see the mythological references and connections in things like
elytra and Icarus, or campfires and Prometheus, but then I find a Quran or
Tanakh in a chest and it's used for a random enchanted book?

But it's worth it, and that's without detailing all the tiny changes like
disabling creeper spawning on the surface overworld, the beautiful new plant
textures, making leather armor reasonable, and so much more.
